<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">North Rhine-Westphalia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">North Rhine-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> is the most populous state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>. It was created by the British occupation authorities in 1946 from the northern part of the Prussian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/province-of-westphalia">Province of Westphalia</a>, and the Free State of Lippe joined it in 1947. It includes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> industrial region and the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, Dortmund and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia.jpg" alt="North Rhine-Westphalia" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bundesland Nordrhein-Westfalen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Rhine-Westphalia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>North Rhine-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> is a landlocked state in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It covers the plains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-rhine">Lower Rhine</a> region and parts of the Central Uplands up to the gorge of Porta Westfalica, and it borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> flows through it, and its banks are heavily populated and industrialised, particularly around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>. The state is not culturally uniform: customs differ significantly between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> and the regions of Westphalia and Lippe, and its flag combines their arms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first written account of the area is by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, who conquered the tribes on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>; the Eburones lived west of the river, and east of it he reported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubii">Ubii</a> and the Sugambri. Augustus established fortified posts on the Rhine, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> never won a firm footing on the right bank. The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a> took place near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> is thought to have spent considerable time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a>, with his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars">Saxon Wars</a> partly fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>.</p><p>The British military administration established the state on 23 August 1946 by merging the Prussian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> and the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a>. The former state of Lippe was added on 21 January 1947, and the state joined the Federal Republic in 1949. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> served as federal capital until the reunification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1990 and as seat of government until 1999. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> crisis began in 1958 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> crisis around 1967, ending the era in which the state was known as the land of coal and steel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>North Rhine-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> has more than 18 million inhabitants and is the most populous German state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a> is the capital and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> the largest city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen-cathedral">Aachen Cathedral</a>, Cologne Cathedral, the Zeche Zollverein in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a>, the Augustusburg Palace in Brühl and the Imperial Abbey of Corvey are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a>. The University of Cologne, founded in 1388, is the oldest of its universities, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-van-beethoven">Ludwig van Beethoven</a> was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bonn">Bonn</a> in 1770.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “North Rhine-Westphalia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It joined the Confederation of the Rhine and the German Empire, and became a Free State in 1918 before merging into <b>North Rhine-Westphalia</b> in 1947.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-lippe">County of Lippe</a></li><li><blockquote>The town of Rietberg is now in <b>North Rhine-Westphalia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-rietberg">County of Rietberg</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1815 it passed to Prussia, and Düsseldorf is now capital of <b>North Rhine-Westphalia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-berg">Duchy of Berg</a></li><li><blockquote>France took the left bank in 1795, and after 1815 the region returned to Prussia; it is now part of <b>North Rhine-Westphalia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-cleves">Duchy of Cleves</a></li><li><blockquote>The Congress of Vienna transferred it to Prussia in 1816, and it became part of the Prussian Province of Westphalia; it now lies in <b>North Rhine-Westphalia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-westphalia">Duchy of Westphalia</a></li><li><blockquote>Rather than joining Lower Saxony, it opted in January 1947 to join <b>North Rhine-Westphalia</b>, under guarantees known as the Lippe Punctations.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-lippe">Free State of Lippe</a></li></ul></section>
